-- Window functions
Unlike ordinary aggregate functions: GROUP BY -> collapses several rows into one result row
-> A window function performs a calculation across a set of related rows without collapsing those rows into a single row

employees
id  name      department  salary
1  | Rahul  | IT         | 60000
2  | Priya  | IT         | 80000
3  | Arun   | HR         | 50000
4  | Sneha  | HR         | 70000

SELECT department, AVG(salary)
FROM employees
GROUP BY department; -- Result enti??

--  Winmdow functionsthoo
SELECT
    name,department, salary,
    AVG(salary) OVER(PARTITION BY department) AS dept_avg_salary
FROM employees;

Rahul | IT | 60000 | 70000
Priya | IT | 80000 | 70000
Arun  | HR | 50000 | 60000
Sneha | HR | 70000 | 60000 -- Every employee row remains

GROUP BY = combines rows
Window Function = keeps rows + calculates across related rows

window_function()
OVER (
    [PARTITION BY column]
    [ORDER BY column]
    [ROWS/RANGE frame_clause]
)

SELECT
    employee_name,
    department,
    salary,
    AVG(salary) OVER(
        PARTITION BY department
    ) AS avg_salary
FROM employees; -- OVER() clause tells Perform this calculation using a window of rows

OVER (
    PARTITION BY ...
    ORDER BY ...
    ROWS ...
)

A. PARTITION BY: Divides rows into separate groups
Example:
PARTITION BY department antee
IT employees - one window
HR employees - another window
Finance employees - another window
-- It is similar to GROUP BY but doesn't collapse the rows

B. ORDER BY: Controls the order in which rows are evaluated
ORDER BY salary DESC means highest salary first

C. Frame: Controls exactly which rows around the current row participate
Example: ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW -- means:Current row + previous 2 rows
This becomes especially useful for running totals, moving averages, and time-series analysis 

